So they're just, they're ruthless with it. Yeah, yeah. And he also recognizes that there's a lot of these plum jobs where people do nothing, but there's a lot of underpaid positions in this part of the government. And he wants to make parks fun above all. He wants to make roads that are good.
所以他们对待这事极其冷酷无情。没错，确实如此。与此同时他也意识到，政府部门里有很多这种拿钱不干活的肥差，但同时也有很多薪酬过低的职位。然而，他首要的目标是让公园变得好玩，他想修建优质的道路。

200
01:17:46.000 --> 01:18:06.000
He wants to have good plans for these things. And, you know, the architects and, you know, engineers of this time, you know, are, a lot of them are out of work. He wants to attract the best of them, but the jobs, the salaries are set so low that, and they're set so low because of some sort of like different federal guidelines and things like trying to-
他希望为这些项目制定出色的规划。而且，当时许多建筑师和工程师都处于失业状态。他希望吸引其中最优秀的人才，但这些职位的薪水被定得极低，而之所以定得这么低，是因为受到各种不同的联邦准则和尝试的限制——

201
01:18:06.000 --> 01:18:26.000
Yeah, the Civil Works Administration is like, we can't spend too much money on this. So, you know, it can be paid more than this amount. And he's just like, screw all that. I need to get the best people to do this. And so he begins finagling his own sort of system of how to pay people so he can get the best and get these people that instead of having, you know
没错，民政工程署（Civil Works Administration）的态度是：“我们不能在这个上面花太多钱，所以薪酬不能超过这个标准。”而他的反应则是：“去他的规章制度，我需要最优秀的人来做这件事。”于是他开始自行变通，设计出一套发薪酬的系统，这样他就能招揽到顶尖人才，从而不再——

202
01:18:26.000 --> 01:18:42.000
a foot deep of things that have been, you know undone and neglected and done poorly he wants to start working through this. And so he's just trying to solve it in every way possible. Yeah. And he has this kind of cattle call for architects. He wants to hire 600 architects. And this is another occupation that's been hit hard. Like you said, you know
面对一英尺厚、那些已被搁置、荒废且做得一塌糊涂的烂摊子，他想要着手把这些工作逐一推进。因此，他正试图通过各种可能的方式来解决问题。没错。而且他为建筑师们举行了一场类似“牛群大召集”的招聘会。他想招募600名建筑师。建筑师这也是一个遭受重创的行业。就像你刚才说的——

203
01:18:42.000 --> 01:19:07.000
if the depression is going on people are not building things. And so if you're an architect or an engineer you're out of work you can't make any money and this is a time when there's no unemployment right now there's no unemployment insurance like these are all things that are being introduced during this time and so people from all over they flock to this kind of cattle call and in one day in January people are there from dawn until the early evening interviewing for jobs.
如果经济大萧条正在肆虐，人们就不会大兴土木。所以如果你是个建筑师或工程师，就会失业，赚不到钱。而且那是一个还没有失业救济金的时代——眼下还没有失业保险这回事，所有这些都是在这一时期才陆续推出的。因此，来自各行各业的人们蜂拥而至，参加这种像“牛群大召集”一样的面试。在1月的一天里，人们从黎明一直排到傍晚，排队面试工作。

204
01:19:07.000 --> 01:19:19.000
If you meet most qualifications, you're put to work that day and they send you to the basement and they say, that's your desk. There's a cot next to it. Work on this plan. Don't leave, sleep here until it's done. We need those blueprints ready.
如果你符合大部分条件，当天就能上岗。他们会把你领到地下室，对你说：“那是你的办公桌。旁边有张行军床。赶紧做这个规划。不准离开，在这儿睡觉，直到做完为止。我们需要这些蓝图马上准备好。”

205
01:19:19.000 --> 01:19:45.000
And he's also unhappy with the workers that have been doing this refurbishing job in the parks. So he says to contractors who he knows, send me your toughest ramrods. These are the people whose job is just to bully people into working harder, I guess. I guess they're supervisors. He goes, send me your toughest ramrods. I'm gonna have them backed up by police officers. We're gonna force everybody to work harder. And they're working through the winter. And this is winter in New York. Now, Roman, you're a northern California guy. The winters there are pretty mild, right?
而且，他对之前一直在公园里从事修缮工作的那些工人很不满意。于是他对相熟的承包商说：“把你们手下最强悍的工头派给我。”这些人的工作，我猜就是靠蛮横霸道逼迫别人更努力地工作吧。我猜他们就是监工。他对承包商说：“把你们最强悍的工头派给我，我要让警察在后面给他们撑腰。我们要强迫所有人加倍努力地干活。”他们在严冬中日夜赶工。这可是纽约的冬天。罗曼，你是个北加州人，那里的冬天相当温和吧？

206
01:19:45.000 --> 01:19:56.000
They're very mild, yeah. Yeah. I live in Southern California now. The winter's here. It just means rain sometimes, but this is New York. Winter is a real thing there. And the winter of 1934, when this is all happening, is severe.
非常温和，是的。是的，我现在住在南加州。这里的冬天，有时意味着下雨，但这里可是纽约。那里的冬天可是动真格的。1934年的冬天，也就是这一切发生的时候，极其严寒。

207
01:19:56.000 --> 01:20:25.000
The city gets 52 inches of snow, Caro says. The mean temperature for February is 11.5 degrees. And Moses has people working in eight-hour shifts, three shifts a day, through the night, in the cold, to get these projects done. On February 22nd, it's in those 18 inches, they keep working through it. And they're working so hard that they're actually working ahead of plans, and the architects have to keep changing their blueprints to accommodate work that's being done, things that are being dug, pipes that are being laid, because it's happening so fast.
卡罗（Caro）写道，这座城市迎来了52英寸（约1.3米）的降雪。2月份的平均气温仅为11.5华氏度（约零下11.4摄氏度）。摩西却让工人分成八小时一班、一天三班倒，冒着严寒彻夜赶工，以求完成这些项目。2月22日，在积雪深达18英寸（约46厘米）的恶劣天气下，他们依然在继续施工。他们的工作进度如此之快，以至于实际施工甚至超前于规划图纸，建筑师们不得不不断修改蓝图，以适应正在进行的施工进度——比如正在开挖的工程、正在铺设的管道，因为一切都发生得太快了。

208
01:20:25.000 --> 01:20:41.000
And one of the big projects, which we'll spend more time on a little bit later in the chapter, is a new Central Park Zoo to replace this menagerie full of lions that you have to shoot in case they escape. And the architects finished the plans for the zoo in 16 days, because they're working so, just so many hours and they're working so hard on it.
其中一个重点项目是新建中央公园动物园，我们在本章稍后会花更多时间来讲述它。它将取代那个装满了狮子、一旦狮子逃跑就不得不将其射杀的旧动物园。建筑师们仅用了16天就完成了动物园的设计图纸，因为他们工作的时间实在太长、太拼了。

209
01:20:41.000 --> 01:21:13.000
It's amazing. The first culmination of this, Saturday, May 1st, 1934, for anyone who has lived in New York, you know that when spring hits in New York City, it is like the world has turned over. Everything feels so different. Everyone comes out kind of like their eyes blinking because they can't, the sun is so bright, you know, they're all wearing coats and they just shed them. It like, people are just shedding clothes in the street because they're not used to the heat that's coming up. And New Yorkers emerged from the winter doldrums to find that out of this 1,800 park renovation projects, 1,700 have been completed.
这太惊人了。这一切的第一个高潮出现在1934年5月1日，星期六。任何在纽约生活过的人都知道，当春天降临纽约市时，那种感觉就像是整个世界都翻天覆地了。一切都变得如此不同。人们纷纷走出家门，由于阳光太刺眼，他们眨巴着眼睛；每个人都穿着大衣，然后又把它们脱掉。人们简直是在街上边走边脱衣服，因为他们还不习惯随之而来的热浪。纽约人从冬日的沉闷中走出来，惊讶地发现，在这1800个公园翻新项目中，竟然已经完成了1700个。

210
01:21:13.000 --> 01:21:34.000
And there's a, hold on, we're already going long, but I'm going to just say a little bit of this description here. It says, just to give the scale of it, every structure and every park in the city had been repainted. Every tennis court had been resurfaced. Every lawn had been reseeded. Eight antiquated golf courses had been reshaped. Eleven miles of bridle paths rebuilt. Thirty-eight miles of walks repaved.
等等，我们已经聊得很久了，但我还是要念一小段这里的描述。书上说，为了让人直观感受到它的规模：市内的每一个结构和每一个公园都被重新粉刷过。每一个网球场都重新铺设了表面。每一片草坪都重新播了草籽。8个陈旧的高尔夫球场被重新塑造。11英里（约17.7公里）的骑马小径被重建。38英里（约61.2公里）步道被重新铺设。

211
01:21:34.000 --> 01:21:58.000
A hundred and forty-five comfort stations renovated. Two hundred and eighty-four statues refurbished. Six hundred and seventy-eight drinking fountains repaired. Seven thousand waste paper baskets replaced. Twenty-two thousand five hundred benches re-slatted, 7,000 dead trees removed, 11,000 new ones planted in their place, and 62,000 others pruned, 86 miles of fencing, most of it unnecessary, torn down, and 19 miles of new fencing installed in its place.
145个舒适站（公共厕所）被翻新。284座雕塑被修缮。678个饮水机被修复。7000个废纸篓被更换。22500条长椅被重新装上板条，7000棵死树被移除，11000棵新树取而代之，还有62000棵树木被修剪，86英里（约138.4公里）的栅栏（其中大部分是不必要的）被拆除，19英里（约30.6公里）的新栅栏取而代之。

212
01:21:58.000 --> 01:22:23.000
Just like... Necessary fencing, I suppose. Yeah. The unnecessary fencing, I have to assume around things that are like damaged that people can't use anymore. But, and the parks look better than they have in at least a generation. And for the first time in years, the USS Maine Memorial on Columbus Circle, which is still there, you know, I used to walk by it all the time when I worked in that area, is for the first time that anyone can remember. It's clean and has all its pieces like that. Part
简直就像……我想，是指那些必要的栅栏吧。是的。至于那些不必要的栅栏，我不得不猜测，它们大概是围着那些人们已经无法再使用的损毁设施。总之，公园看起来比至少整整一代人以来的样子都要好。多年来，哥伦布圆环上的缅因号战舰纪念碑（USS Maine Memorial）——它现在还在那儿，我以前在那个区域工作时经常路过它——这是人们记忆中破天荒头一次，它变得干干净净，而且所有的部件都完好无缺。这座雕塑的一部分

213
01:22:23.000 --> 01:22:42.000
of that statue is a little boy and the arms of that boy had been missing for so long. And for movie fans, this is where in the movie Taxi Driver, Robert De Niro's character, Travis Bickle, is about to assassinate a presidential candidate and then runs off when he gets noticed. And so you have to imagine that scene loses some of its power, maybe it gains some, I don't know. If the statue behind Charles Palatine,
是一个小男孩，那个男孩的双臂已经失踪了很久。对于电影迷来说，电影《出租车司机》（Taxi Driver）中，罗伯特·德尼罗（Robert De Niro）饰演的角色特拉维斯·比克尔（Travis Bickle）正准备刺杀总统候选人，但在被人发现后仓皇逃跑，场景就是在这里。所以你可以想象一下，如果候选人查尔斯·帕拉丁（Charles Palatine）身后的那座雕塑

214
01:22:42.000 --> 01:22:59.000
the candidate, is just wrecked, is just in bad shape. That's right. And this is happening all over the city. Parks all over the city are getting refurbished. They're getting to a state where people can really enjoy them again. And this is again, why that whole last chapter spent so much time on this.
破烂不堪、状况糟糕，那个场景的冲击力可能会减弱一些，也许会增强一些，我也不知道。就是这样。而且这种情况在全城各地都在上演。全市各地的公园都在翻新。它们的状况恢复到了人们可以真正再次享受它们的程度。这也是为什么上一章要花那么多时间来讲述这一切的原因所在。

215
01:22:59.000 --> 01:23:15.000
It's to show you how far the city had come and how far it had come because of Robert Moses. Caro is presenting a real Moses before and after on this city. That this city is, there's a real rebirth in the parks facilities. And there will be again with the roads later on, but I wanted to highlight two things.
这是为了向你展示这座城市发生了多大的变化，以及因为它而带来的巨大改变。卡罗在展示这座城市在罗伯特·摩西前后的真实对比。这座城市迎来了公园设施的真正重生。后来道路建设也将迎来同样的重生，但我现在想重点强调两件事。

216
01:23:15.000 --> 01:23:31.000
One of the things they did is they rebuilt the Prospect Park Zoo in Brooklyn, which I'm very thankful for because I went to that zoo many times. I took my children there probably a hundred times. It's a great little zoo. And reading this, I was like, oh yeah, like Robert Moses didn't just cause problems in my life
其中一件是他们重建了布鲁克林的展望公园动物园（Prospect Park Zoo），对此我非常感激，因为我去过那个动物园很多次。我带我的孩子们去那里大概有一百次。那是一个很棒的小动物园。读到这里时我想：“哦，对啊，罗伯特·摩西不仅在我们的生活中制造了麻烦——”

217
01:23:31.000 --> 01:23:50.000
about round mass transit. Like he also did things that were still positive in my life years later. Like Bryant Park, which is a like a beautiful, necessary park was garbage. And then they refurbished it. They restore Central Park. They remove the shantytown, they evict the deformed sheep, they kill 230,000 rats,
关于糟糕的公共交通系统。“他在多年后依然在我的生活中留下了积极的影响。”比如布莱恩特公园（Bryant Park），这是一个美丽且必不可少的公园，过去曾是一片垃圾场，后来他们将其翻新。他们修复了中央公园。他们清除了贫民窟，驱逐了那些畸形的羊，消灭了23万只老鼠。

218
01:23:50.000 --> 01:24:13.000
and they build new equipments and facilities, and they build the restaurant Tavern on the Green where 40 some odd years later, my father proposed to my mother before they got married, leading to me. So, Martin Rose is very responsible for me at this point, for my existence, you know? And the city doesn't have money to buy new land for parks. Even the land is very cheap right now during the Depression. So Moses sends one of his men, a man named Bill Latham,
他们建造了新的设备和设施，他们建造了“绿茵台”餐厅（Tavern on the Green）——大约四十年后，我的父亲正是在那里向我的母亲求婚，随后他们结婚，才有了我。所以，从某种意义上说，罗伯特·摩西对我的存在负有重大责任，对吧？当时纽约市根本没有钱为公园购买新的土地。尽管在大萧条时期土地非常便宜，但摩西还是派了他的手下一名叫比尔·拉瑟姆（Bill Latham）的人去——

219
01:24:13.000 --> 01:24:53.000
he goes, inventory every piece of publicly owned land in the city, any city department that owns a piece of land, go find out about it, look at it personally, see if it's being used. And they find just a ton of unused or abandoned land all over the city and most able to convince the Guardia to give the parks department almost all of that land. And he starts plans right away for 69 parks and playgrounds in slum areas. And they redevelop this whole multi-block downtown plot that is now Sara Delano Roosevelt Park, which I mentioned just because as an NYU student in downtown, like if I was going a little bit further downtown, I walked through that park all the time. Like I, you can walk through the city and still see so many footprints of Robert Moses all over
他命令道：“去盘点城里每一块属于公共所有的土地，任何拥有土地的市政部门，去把它们找出来，亲自去查看，看看它们是否得到了利用。”结果他们全城到处都发现了大量闲置或废弃的土地，而且大部分都说服了市长拉瓜迪亚（LaGuardia）将几乎所有这些土地都划拨给公园部门。他立即着手规划在贫民窟地区建设69个公园和游乐场。他们重新开发了市中心这个横跨好几个街区的地块，也就是现在的萨拉·德拉诺·罗斯福公园（Sara Delano Roosevelt Park）。我提到它，只是因为作为纽约大学的学生，如果我要去市中心更往南的地方，我经常会穿过那个公园。你走在纽约这座城市里，到处都还能看到罗伯特·摩西留下的无数足迹。

220
01:24:53.000 --> 01:25:36.000
the place. And he's so knowledgeable about the law and about public funding mechanisms that even when it, it seems like there's no money, he's always finding money. He's like, oh yeah, there's all this land that the state owns in the city that they forgot about. Give it to us. He's like, wasn't there a memorial fund for World War I memorial that never got built? Well, we'll build war memorial playgrounds. We'll use that money. He's like, didn't Arnold Rothstein, the mobster, when he died, didn't he have a lot of unpaid back taxes, but he owns some land? And so he goes to Arnold Rothstein's estate and he's like, well, in exchange for this land, we'll forgive the back taxes. Like he's constantly wheedling and finding ways to get what he wants. He goes to the Catholic Church and the gas company and all these rich philanthropists and he's like, hey, don't you want to give us some land for some parks and playgrounds?
而且他对法律和公共资金筹措机制如此精通，以至于即使在似乎无钱可用的情况下，他也能总能找到钱。他会说：“哦，对了，州政府在市内拥有所有这些他们已经遗忘的土地。把它交给我们。”他还会说：“难道不是有一个从未建成的第一次世界大战纪念碑的筹款基金吗？好吧，我们就用这笔钱来建造战争纪念游乐场。”他甚至会说：“阿诺德·罗斯坦（Arnold Rothstein），那个黑帮分子死的时候，难道没有留下很多欠缴的税款吗？但他名下有一些土地。”于是他去找阿诺德·罗斯坦的遗产管理人说：“好吧，用这块地来交换，我们可以免除这些滞纳的税款。”他不断地巧取豪夺，寻找各种方法来达到目的。他去找天主教会、天然气公司和所有这些富有的大慈善家说：“嘿，难道你们不想给我们捐点土地来建公园和游乐场吗？”

221
01:25:36.000 --> 01:25:54.000
Wouldn't that be wonderful? And the press is orgasmic about all of this. They just love him. He's in the New York Times nearly once a day for all of 1934, which is certainly more times than Albert Einstein to use Caro's earlier metric for how many times Moses is in the paper. Although actually in this chapter, I think he mentions that he's in the paper
“那不是很棒吗？”媒体对这一切简直狂热到了极点。他们爱死他了。在整个1934年，他几乎每天都会登上《纽约时报》一次。用卡罗之前衡量摩西上报频率的标准来看，这绝对比阿尔伯特·爱因斯坦（Albert Einstein）上报的次数还要多。不过事实上在这一章里，我想他提到摩西上报的频率——

222
01:25:54.000 --> 01:26:08.000
more often than J. Edgar Hoover. That's the metric he uses for this chapter. Yeah, yeah. It is really remarkable. And again, this is like one of these things where you just like get through this and you think, you know, if I was a person around at that time, I would be just like,
比J·埃德加·这是他在这一章中使用的衡量标准。真的非常引人注目。而且，这又是那种让人不禁感叹的事情：如果你是那个时代生活在那里的人，你肯定会——

223
01:26:08.000 --> 01:26:30.000
I would have a like Robert Moses foam fangirl. I mean, I would just be all about all of this. And it is really stunning. And I love these ways that he gets around things like using the war memorial fund to in building the war memorial playgrounds and just, he just puts a plaque that just says, you know, war, remember it.
成为罗伯特·摩西的狂热崇拜者。我的意思是，我绝对会全力支持这一切。这确实太震撼了。我特别喜欢他绕过障碍的方式，比如利用战争纪念基金来修建战争纪念游乐场，然后只需要立一块牌子，上面写着：“战争，请铭记它。”

224
01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:46.000
In honor of those we lost. We did it, great. Bring in the playground equipment. But it's something that I'd be curious to talk to a historian of parks, I guess, about whether that started the idea of public parks having war memorials embedded in them. Cause there's certainly a lot of them now.
“为了纪念那些逝者。我们做到了，太棒了。把游乐场器材搬进来吧。”不过，我很好奇，想跟一位公园历史学家探讨一下，这是否开启了“公共公园内嵌战争纪念设施”这种理念的先河？因为现在这样的公园确实有很多。

225
