This comes up a lot in the book because Robert Moses is like a graduate thesis was pretty much a treatise on why people like Al Smith shouldn't be governor, but they become friends and then he amasses so much power because of the support of Al Smith. And you know, contrary to that, Al Smith, you know, pretty much agreed with Roosevelt on almost everything, his successor, but when the New Deal was enacted, Al Smith was just completely against it, even though it sort of contained all the values he seemed to have when he was governing, just because by that point, he was mad at Roosevelt.
这本书里经常提到这一点，因为罗伯特·摩西的研究生毕业论文几乎就是一篇论述为什么像阿尔·史密斯这样的人不应该当州长的论文，但他们却成了朋友，随后他又借助阿尔·史密斯的鼎力支持积累了极其庞大的权力。与此相反，阿尔·史密斯（你知道的）几乎在所有事情上都同意他的继任者罗斯福的看法，但当“新政”颁布时，阿尔·史密斯却坚决反对，尽管它包含了他在执政时似乎所拥护的所有价值观，仅仅是因为到了那个时候，他生罗斯福的气了。

300
02:03:59.000 --> 02:04:59.000
Like he didn't like Roosevelt, and so he hated the New Deal. And it just is like over and over again, you see this in this book, and it sounds like that really rings true to today as well. Oh yeah. I have seen, I mean, it's terrible. It is awful. And you know, it's not to say that everybody operates in this way, but when we think about governance, there are hundreds of members of Congress, there are thousands of people in positions of decision making power, and some of them are going to be spiteful and like human nature. And I have seen people make terrible or hurtful decisions because they had an axe to grind. And especially kind of, you know, the more you are in places of elevated
就像他不喜欢罗斯福，所以他恨屋及乌地痛恨“新政”一样。你在书中一次又一次地看到这种情况，而且听起来这在今天也同样真实。哦，是的。我见过，我的意思是，这太糟糕了。这太可怕了。你知道，这并不是说所有人都以这种方式行事，但当我们思考治理时，国会有数百名成员，有数千人处于拥有决策权的位置，其中一些人会心胸狭隘，这就是人性。我见过人们因为想公报私仇而做出可怕或伤人的决定。特别是那种，你知道的，你越是处于高处

301
